COPY public."ProjectDocument" (id, "projectId", title, content, "docType", version, "createdById", "createdAt", "updatedAt") FROM stdin;
1	10	Agent Skill Lifecycle Brainstorm	# Brainstorm: Blended Agent Skill Lifecycle\n**Project:** Spec Task Board (ID: 10)\n**Status:** Refined / Swarm Architecture\n**Date:** 2026-04-08\n\n## 1. Project Types & Architectural Flexibility\n*   **Structured Epics:** Full workflows (Discovery -> Finalized).\n*   **Freeform:** Simple Todo -> Done.\n*   **Hybrid:** Structured parent Epics spawning sandboxed sub-boards.\n\n## 2. Distributed Agent Swarm (Specialized Local Agents)\nWe leverage the WebSocket feed and OpenFang/OpenClaw to resource a swarm of small, local agents.\n\n*   **Research Agent:** Triggered by `Plan`. Uses `code_index` to pre-fetch context. Logs `📁 [FILES TO REVIEW]`.\n*   **Critic Agent:** Triggered by `Verify`. Compares code diffs against `SPECIFICATION.md`.\n*   **Memory Agent:** Inspired by `OpenMemory`. Distills long comment threads into lean `BRAINSTORM` artifacts.\n\n## 3. The Execution Protocol (Mermaid Flow)\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant Hub as Kanban Hub (Agent API)\n    participant Fang as OpenFang (WebSocket Listener)\n    participant Local as Local Critic Agent\n    participant Cloud as Cloud Orchestrator\n\n    Hub->>Fang: [WS Event] Task 51 -> Verify\n    Fang->>Hub: GET /tasks/51?inline=true\n    Fang->>Local: "Verify these changes against Spec"\n    Local-->>Hub: POST /comments "Review Results"\n    Note over Hub: If failure, Hub patches Task back to Execute\n```\n\n## 4. Refined Agent API Call Stack\n*   `GET /api/agent/projects/:id/tasks/:id?inline=true&compact=true` (Context Acquisition)\n*   `POST /api/agent/projects/:id/docs` (Knowledge Commitment)\n*   `POST /api/agent/projects/:id/tasks/:id/comments` (Work Logging)\n*   `POST /api/agent/projects/:id/tasks/:id/doc-links` (Context Binding)	BRAINSTORM	3	1	2026-04-08 11:31:28.089	2026-04-08 13:41:19.582
2	10	Project Constitution	# SpecTask Constitution\n\n## 1. Quality Standards\n- **Safety First:** No agent shall modify `.env` or system credentials.\n- **Atomic Changes:** Implementation should be surgical (1 file at a time).\n- **Validation Required:** No task moves to "Finalized" without a verified `TEST_REPORT.md`.\n- **Rust Standards:** All Rust code MUST pass `cargo fmt`, `cargo clippy` (with zero warnings), and `cargo test` before submission.\n\n## 2. Git Governance\n- **Pre-commit Hooks:** All Rust-based repositories must use the project-standard `.git/hooks/pre-commit` script to enforce quality gates locally.\n- **Branching:** Work should occur in task-specific branches (e.g., `feat/SPEC-1-scaffold`).\n\n## 3. Communication Rules\n- Agents must use the `internal_monologue` for technical reasoning.\n- Public `comments` are reserved for human-facing summaries.\n- All "Epics" must have a linked `SPECIFICATION.md` before work begins.\n\n## 4. Tech Stack Mandates\n- **Backend:** Node.js / Prisma / Express.\n- **Frontend:** Vue 3 / Tailwind 4 / DaisyUI 5.\n- **Agent Skill:** Rust Binary (Compiled) using `rust-mcp-sdk` (pmcp).	CONSTITUTION	3	1	2026-04-08 11:36:56.995	2026-04-09 05:34:00.66
3	10	Rust MCP Orchestrator Spec	# Technical Spec: Rust MCP\n\n- Binary: `mcp-vibe-bridge`\n- Protocol: MCP 1.0\n- Auth: Delegate Key Only	SPECIFICATION	1	1	2026-04-08 13:03:27.006	2026-04-08 13:03:27.006
4	10	Product Platform Roadmap	# Roadmap: The Vibe Tasks Product Platform\n**Status:** Architecture Finalized / Universal Binary Ready\n**Version:** 1.3.0 (Universal Swarm Edition)\n**Date:** 2026-04-10\n\n## 1. The "Logic Hub" Architecture\nTo support both high-end IDE agents and local shell scripts, the VibeTask Orchestrator is organized as a Rust Workspace:\n*   **`vibe-core`**: Shared library containing the Hub API client and battle-hardened algorithms (Shrimp/SpecKit).\n*   **`vibe-mcp`**: High-autonomy sidecar for Cursor/Claude.\n*   **`vibe-cli`**: Lower-autonomy tool for terminal users, OpenFang laborers, and cron jobs.\n\n## 2. Event-Driven Swarm Protocol\nThe platform acts as a shared nervous system for specialized agents.\n1.  **Lobby (IRC):** For human-observable reasoning and bot-to-bot consensus.\n2.  **Backbone (NATS.io):** For high-speed task claiming and machine data.\n3.  **Runtime (OpenFang):** For resource management and secure execution of the labor swarm.\n\n## 3. The "Cron-to-Lattice" Pattern\nSustainable automation is achieved by leveraging standard system utilities:\n*   **Injection:** Linux `cron` uses `vibe-cli` to inject recurring tasks into the Hub.\n*   **Observation:** The swarm listens to the Hub feed and autonomous laborers pick up the work.\n*   **Verification:** The binary's scoring algorithm ensures automated jobs meet quality standards.\n\n## 4. Implementation Roadmap\n\n### Phase 1: Platform Foundation (Current)\n- [x] Standardize Agent API prefix and `/me` tiering.\n- [x] Implement `?inline=true` JIT context packet.\n- [x] Ratify Master Manifest and Internal Logic Blueprint.\n\n### Phase 2: Technical Scaffolding (Next)\n- [ ] Initialize Rust Workspace (`vibe-core`, `vibe-mcp`, `vibe-cli`).\n- [ ] Implement `vibe-cli task create/move/context` commands.\n- [ ] Implement TOML-based Identity Registry.\n\n### Phase 3: Swarm Integration (Advanced)\n- [ ] Implement Vibe-Relay (Socket.IO to NATS/IRC).\n- [ ] Bolt VibeMCP into OpenFang as a Tool Provider.\n- [ ] Add "Mission Support" (YAML-driven state machines).\n	BRAINSTORM	2	1	2026-04-08 15:55:11.933	2026-04-10 19:19:17.816
5	10	Project Type & Formality Profiles	# Project Type & Formality Profiles\n\nVibe Tasks supports varying levels of "Lattice Rigidity" to accommodate different workflows.\n\n## 1. Type: Product Design (High Formality)\n**Target:** New feature development, complex systems.\n**Rigidity:** Level 5 (Strict State Machine).\n**Rules:**\n- All tasks must move through all 6 Lifecycle columns.\n- Mandatory `SPECIFICATION.md` before Planning.\n- Human `accept-plan` required to spawn sub-boards.\n- Verification requires independent `Critic Agent` sign-off.\n\n## 2. Type: Sys-Admin / Ops (Low Formality)\n**Target:** Helpdesk, server maintenance, one-off tickets.\n**Rigidity:** Level 1 (Freeform).\n**Rules:**\n- Minimal columns: `Todo`, `Doing`, `Done`.\n- No required artifacts.\n- Tasks are usually "Leaf Nodes" (no recursion).\n- Agents act as automated assistants (e.g., "Renew SSL cert") rather than architects.\n\n## 3. Type: Hybrid (Dynamic Formality)\n**Target:** Ongoing project with both maintenance and feature work.\n**Rigidity:** Context-Dependent.\n**Rules:**\n- Main board is Freeform.\n- Epic cards (Containers) trigger a "Formality Escalation" once drilled into.\n- The sub-board enforces the Product Design workflow for that specific feature.\n\n## 4. Implementation Idea: Formality Templates\nProject settings should allow the owner to select a "Formality Profile" which automatically:\n1. Initializes the correct columns.\n2. Sets the `Agent Prompts` for those columns.\n3. Configures the `POST /move` validation logic.	SPECIFICATION	1	1	2026-04-08 15:56:01.699	2026-04-08 15:56:01.699
6	10	Case Study: Recurring Automation	# Case Study: Recurring Automation & Job Queues\n\n## Overview\nThis profile transforms the Vibe Tasks board into a high-throughput **Automation Hub**. It leverages specialized local agents (resourced via OpenFang/OpenClaw) to claim, research, and execute recurring system tasks.\n\n## 1. The Automation Lifecycle (Columns)\n\n| Column | Goal | Agent Action |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1. Job Queue** | Bounty | Listen for WebSocket event. Claim task by assigning `delegateId`. |\n| **2. Research** | Validation | Agent analyzes the specific request. Logs `📁 [FILES TO REVIEW]`. |\n| **3. WIP** | Execution | Surgical implementation or system action. Logs `📁 [FILES TOUCHED]`. |\n| **4. Audit** | Review | Human or Critic Agent verifies the automated result. |\n| **5. Archive** | Archive | Task persists for historical visibility. Spawns next recurrence. |\n\n## 2. The Recurrence Engine\nTo support sustainable automation, the platform implements a cloning mechanism:\n*   **Trigger:** Task with `isRecurring: true` moves to the `Archive` column.\n*   **Action:** The backend creates a shallow clone of the task.\n*   **Result:** A fresh, unassigned version of the task is placed back in the **Job Queue**.\n\n## 3. Distributed "Claim" Logic\nTo prevent agent collisions in a multi-threaded swarm:\n1.  The Hub broadcasts a `TASK_CREATED` event.\n2.  All listening agents (OpenFang instances) receive the notification.\n3.  The first agent to successfully `PATCH` the `assigneeId` wins the task.\n4.  Subsequent PATCH attempts from other agents are rejected with `409 Conflict`.\n\n## 4. Integration with Local Tooling\n*   **OpenFang:** Listens to the WS feed and maps Kanban state changes to local model invocations.\n*   **Code Index:** Used during the **Research** phase to gather the necessary file context without human intervention.\n	SPECIFICATION	1	1	2026-04-08 16:18:53.72	2026-04-08 16:18:53.72
7	10	SPEC-1 Implementation Plan	## Core Rust Crate Setup\n## Implement Vibe Client\n## Implement Lattice Logic\n## Implement MCP Server Transport	IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN	1	1	2026-04-08 16:37:20.367	2026-04-08 16:37:20.367
8	10	Agent API Ownership Policy	# Policy: Agent API Ownership & Sovereignty\n**Status:** Approved Specification\n**Version:** 1.0.0\n**Date:** 2026-04-08\n\n## 1. The Sovereignty Matrix\nTo prevent alignment drift and recursive loops, we distinguish between **Governance** (Human) and **Execution** (Agent) capabilities.\n\n| API Category | Action | Owner | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Governance** | Create/Delete Project | Human | Prevents unauthorized resource creation. |\n| **Governance** | Set Constitution | Human | The agent must be subject to the rules, not author. |\n| **Governance** | Accept Plan | Human | Critical gate for spawning labor/sub-tasks. |\n| **Execution** | Draft Spec/Plan | Agent | Primary artifact generation. |\n| **Execution** | Move Task | Agent | Functional gate progression. |\n| **Execution** | Log Work (Comments) | Agent | Transparency and work-tree synchronization. |\n| **Intelligence** | Link Task to Doc | Agent | Maintaining recursive context. |\n\n## 2. Behavioral Guardrails (The "Push-back" Protocol)\nThe Agent MCP Binary must enforce context sanity through the following behaviors:\n\n### A. Context Ambiguity (Multiple Projects)\n*   **The Problem:** User asks "What is the status?" while the agent has access to multiple delegated projects.\n*   **The Rule:** The agent **must** list the accessible project names and ask for a specific "Lens" focus before performing analysis.\n*   **Response Pattern:** *"I see access to 'SpecTask' and 'SysAdmin'. Which project should I analyze?"*\n\n### B. Mandatory Redirection\n*   **The Problem:** User asks the agent to perform a Governance action (e.g., "Delete this project").\n*   **The Rule:** The agent **must** decline and provide direct navigation instructions to the Human UI.\n*   **Response Pattern:** *"I do not have the authority to delete projects. Please navigate to **Project Settings > Danger Zone** to perform this action manually."*\n\n## 3. Optimized Agent Endpoints (The "Aggregator" Stack)\nTo support the Push-back protocol without token bloat, the following endpoints are required in the `/api/agent` prefix:\n\n### `GET /api/agent/projects/summary`\n*   **Returns:** Lean array of `{ id, name, formalityLevel, totalTasks, activeSprints }`.\n*   **Usage:** Used for the "Push-back" list and global status dashboards.\n\n### `GET /api/agent/projects/:id/tasks?compact=true`\n*   **Behavior:** Returns only `id`, `name`, and `statusId` for all tasks in a project.\n*   **Usage:** Allows the agent to calculate "Stats" (e.g., "3 in TODO, 1 in Execute") using minimal tokens.\n\n## 4. Formality Steering (Lattice Metadata)\nProjects carry a `formalityLevel` (1-5) in their metadata. The agent must check this before each turn:\n*   **Level 5 (Product Design):** Enforce SpecKit/Shrimp state machine (Spec -> Plan -> Execute).\n*   **Level 1 (Job Queue):** Skip Design phases; move directly to Execution.\n\n---\n*Ratified for delivery to the Backend and Agent Development teams.*\n	CONSTITUTION	1	1	2026-04-08 18:08:31.527	2026-04-08 18:08:31.527
