44	10	Feature Request: Hierarchy Visibility	# Feature Request: Pre-Acceptance Hierarchy Visibility (UI)\n\n**Component:** Frontend (`KanbanCard.vue`, `TaskDialog.vue`)  \n**Status:** Design Proposal  \n**Priority:** Medium\n\n## 1. Objective\nEnable users to visualize and manage parent-child task relationships *prior* to the formal "Plan Acceptance" gate. Currently, the hierarchical nature of a task is "hidden" until the board explosion is triggered.\n\n## 2. Requirements\n\n### A. Kanban Card Badge\n*   Display a `childCount` badge (e.g., `⑆ 5`) on all cards where `childCount > 0`, regardless of the `planAccepted` status.\n*   **Visual Cue:** Use a distinct icon (e.g., a branch or folder icon) to indicate that this task is a container for sub-tasks.\n\n### B. Task Dialog Preview\n*   Enable the **"Sub-tasks"** section in the Task Dialog for all tasks.\n*   Allow manual creation of child tasks in the `Discovery` and `Specify` phases. \n*   **Rationale:** Users often brainstorm sub-tasks manually before the agent generates a formal `IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md`.\n\n### C. Workspace Switcher Integration\n*   Ensure that any task with children appears in the **Workspace Switcher** (Global Pulldown), even if its plan has not been accepted. Label these as `[DRAFT]` or `[DESIGN]`.\n\n## 3. Rationale\nThis feature prevents "Invisible Work." It allows the human architect to see the emerging structure of an Epic as the agent (or human) populates it with stories during the Discovery phase.\n	SPECIFICATION	1	1	2026-04-12 03:01:35.062	2026-04-12 03:01:35.062
45	10	Bug Report: POST API parentId Omission	# Bug Report: POST API Ignores parentId Field\n\n**Severity:** High (Breaks recursive task creation)\n**Component:** Backend (`src/api/routes/tasks.ts`, `src/api/routes/agent/tasks.ts`)\n\n## 1. Summary\nWhile the `createTaskSchema` includes the `parentId` field, the actual route handlers fail to extract this field from the request body and pass it to the Prisma creation call. This results in all newly created child tasks being orphaned at the top level.\n\n## 2. Evidence\n*   **`src/api/routes/tasks.ts` (Lines 300-350):** The destructuring of `req.body` omits `parentId`.\n*   **`src/api/routes/agent/tasks.ts` (Lines 100-150):** Similar omission in the agent-scoped task creation route.\n\n## 3. Impact\nUsers and agents cannot create nested sub-tasks in a single call. Hierarchical structures must be "fixed" via a subsequent `PATCH` call, leading to state desync and visual clutter on the main board during the window between creation and patching.\n\n## 4. Requested Fix\nUpdate the `router.post('/')` handlers in both human and agent routers to include `parentId` in the destructuring and the Prisma `data` object:\n\n```typescript\n// Proposed Fix\nconst { name, description, projectId, parentId, ... } = body;\n\nconst task = await prisma.task.create({\n  data: {\n    name,\n    description,\n    projectId,\n    parentId: parentId || null,\n    // ...\n  }\n});\n```\n	SPECIFICATION	1	1	2026-04-12 03:47:13.918	2026-04-12 03:47:13.918
46	10	ADR: Hierarchical vs. Linear Expansion	# ADR: Hierarchical vs. Linear Expansion\n\n## Context\nDuring the 19-Phase board expansion for the VibeMCP implementation, the `/accept-plan` feature defaulted to creating all 19 phases as child tasks of the parent epic (Task 100). This resulted in a "Half-Duplex" state where child tasks were hidden but intended to be independent containers themselves.\n\n## Decision\nWe will distinguish between two types of "Board Explosion":\n\n1.  **Hierarchical (Child) Expansion:** Used for decomposing an atomic task into specific implementation steps (Work Packages). These remain nested and hidden from the main board.\n2.  **Linear (Sibling) Expansion:** Used for decomposing a high-level goal into a series of **Epics**. These are promoted to the same hierarchical level as the parent.\n\n## Implementation Detail\nThe 19 Roadmap Phases have been **Promoted** to top-level tasks by setting `parentId: null`. This preserves the project's visual clarity and prevents "Recursive Inception" (3+ levels of nesting).\n\n## Consequence\nThe main board now correctly renders the 19-Phase Strategic Roadmap. Each Phase can now independently serve as its own container for sub-tasks (Task 1.1, 1.2, etc.), maintaining our "One Tier of Nesting" UI constraint.\n	SPECIFICATION	1	1	2026-04-12 04:20:15.563	2026-04-12 04:20:15.563
47	10	[RESOLVED] Bug Report: Agent Detail API Omissions	# Bug Report: Agent Task Detail Omission\n\n**Severity:** Critical (Blocks JIT Context)\n**Component:** Backend (`src/api/routes/agent/tasks.ts`)\n\n## 1. Summary\nThe Agent-scoped task detail endpoint (`GET /api/agent/projects/:projectId/tasks/:taskId`) is significantly less detailed than the standard human task detail endpoint. Specifically, it fails to include `docLinks` and `children`, making it impossible for the VibeMCP sidecar to assemble the required context for the LLM.\n\n## 2. Comparison\n*   **Human API:** Returns full `docLinks` (with nested documents) and the `children` array.\n*   **Agent API:** Returns only base task fields, assignee, and column. `docLinks` and `children` are missing from the JSON response.\n\n## 3. Impact\nThis breaks the **JIT Context Injection** protocol. The Rust binary calls the Agent API with `?inline=true`, but since the backend doesn't even attempt to fetch the links, the agent receives zero documentation context.\n\n## 4. Requested Fix\nUpdate the `prisma.task.findFirst` call in `src/api/routes/agent/tasks.ts` to include the same relations as the human route:\n\n```typescript\n// Required Includes for Agent Task Detail\ninclude: {\n  assignee: { select: { id: true, name: true } },\n  column: { select: { id: true, name: true, type: true } },\n  docLinks: {\n    include: {\n      document: {\n        select: { id: true, title: true, docType: true, content: true, version: true }\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  children: {\n    select: { id: true, name: true, identifier: true, order: true, isContainer: true, planAccepted: true }\n  },\n  _count: { select: { children: true } }\n}\n```\n	SPECIFICATION	2	1	2026-04-12 04:45:15.072	2026-04-12 04:59:50.238
48	10	Bug Report: Frontend Visibility Failure	# Bug Report: Task Dialog Visibility Failure\n\n**Severity:** Medium (Blocks human document access)\n**Component:** Frontend (`TaskDialog.vue`)\n\n## 1. Summary\nThe Task Dialog UI fails to render "Linked Documents" and "Sub-tasks" even when the Backend API correctly returns this data in the JSON response.\n\n## 2. Evidence\n*   **API Response (Task 115):** The JSON contains a populated `docLinks` array with 3 documents and a `children` array with 3 tasks.\n*   **UI Behavior:** The "Linked Documents" section explicitly displays "No linked documents," and the "Sub-tasks" section is entirely missing or empty.\n\n## 3. Potential Root Cause\nThe `TaskDialog.vue` component may be misaligned with the updated API schema:\n1.  **Key Mismatch:** The UI might be expecting `.documents` instead of `.docLinks`.\n2.  **Type Transformation:** The component might fail to parse the nested `document` object inside the `docLinks` array.\n\n## 4. Expected Behavior\n*   The **Linked Documents** section should list all titles found in the `docLinks[].document.title` array.\n*   The **Sub-tasks** section should render a list of all child tasks found in the `children` array.\n	SPECIFICATION	1	1	2026-04-12 13:05:21.872	2026-04-12 13:05:21.872
11	10	Master Manifest: Vibe Product Platform	# Manifest: The Vibe Product Platform\n**Version:** 1.6.0 (Balanced6 Mesh)\n**Status:** Architecture Finalized / Workspace Pivot\n**Date:** 2026-04-12\n\n## 1. Core Vision: The Distributed Lattice\nVibe Tasks is a **High-Fidelity Project OS**. The orchestrator is now architected as a **6-Crate Rust Workspace** to ensure total separation of concerns and zero business-logic duplication.\n\n### The Balanced6 Architecture:\n1.  **`vibetask-core`**: Pure domain logic and types.\n2.  **`vibetask-hub-client`**: Hardened REST/WS integration.\n3.  **`vibetask-app`**: CQRS command/query handlers.\n4.  **`vibetask-tool-catalog`**: Centralized tool metadata and column mappings.\n5.  **`vibetask-mcp`**: Thin transport adapter for IDE bots.\n6.  **`vibetask-cli`**: Thin transport adapter for terminal/cron labor.\n\n## 2. Behavioral Mandate (Stateless Swarm)\n*   **One Source of Truth:** All state lives in the VibeTask Hub.\n*   **Dual-Tier Identity:** Platform (Read-only) vs Project (Labor) tiers detected at handshake.\n*   **JIT Context:** One-call `?inline=true` assembly with strict 4k token budgeting.\n\n---\n*Ratified for Balanced6 Workspace Implementation.*\n	CONSTITUTION	3	1	2026-04-09 12:05:42.433	2026-04-13 13:38:57.378
42	10	[RESOLVED] Bug Report: Agent API Discrepancies (Detailed)	# Bug Report: Incorrect Hub API Routing in MCP Tools\n\n**Severity:** Critical (Blocks all write operations)\n**Component:** `crates/vibetask-app/src/mcp_tools.rs`\n\n## 1. Summary\nThe `spawn_sub_board` tool (and potentially others) is failing with `401 Unauthorized`. Technical analysis confirms that the tool is attempting to call the **Human API** endpoint instead of the **Agent API** endpoint.\n\n## 2. Technical Evidence\nIn `crates/vibetask-app/src/mcp_tools.rs`, the `create_task_via_api` helper method contains the following line:\n\n```rust\n// LINE 2510\nlet url = format!("{}/api/tasks", ctx.api_client.base_url_string());\n```\n\n*   **The Error:** `/api/tasks` is a human-scoped route that requires a `Bearer` token. It ignores the `x-agent-api-key` header.\n*   **The Fix:** Agents MUST use the agent-scoped project route:\n    `POST /api/agent/projects/{project_id}/tasks`\n\n## 3. Required Correction\nUpdate the helper method to use the correct agent-scoped path:\n\n```rust\n// Proposed Correction\nlet url = format!(\n    "{}/api/agent/projects/{}/tasks", \n    ctx.api_client.base_url_string(),\n    request.project_id\n);\n```\n\n## 4. Impact\nUntil this is fixed, agents cannot create sub-tasks, link documents, or perform any write operations, as they are being incorrectly routed to the human-only validation layer.\n	SPECIFICATION	3	1	2026-04-11 23:33:50.052	2026-04-14 03:22:27.919
73	10	Protocol: Auditor Hand-off & Circular Workflow	# Auditor Hand-off Protocol: Verified API Scenarios\n**Status:** Verified / Behavioral Contract\n**Version:** 1.0.0\n**Date:** 2026-04-14\n\n## 1. Overview\nThis document records the verified API interactions for multi-agent coordination on the Vibe Lattice. It defines the "Hand-off" from a Laborer agent to an Auditor agent and the subsequent "Circular Workflow" (Rejection/Escalation).\n\n## 2. Test Scenario 1: Successful Labor Hand-off\n**Actors:** `AgentSmith` (Laborer) -> `GateKeeper` (Auditor)\n\n### Call A: Laborer moves task to Verify\n```bash\ncurl -sS -X PATCH http://localhost:3000/api/agent/projects/10/tasks/101 \\n  -H 'x-agent-api-key: [AGENT_SMITH_KEY]' \\n  -d '{"columnId": 54}'\n```\n**Response:** `{"name": "Agent API Endpoints", "column": "Verify"}`\n\n### Call B: Auditor performs Finalization\n```bash\ncurl -sS -X PATCH http://localhost:3000/api/agent/projects/10/tasks/101 \\n  -H 'x-agent-api-key: [GATEKEEPER_KEY]' \\n  -d '{"columnId": 55}'\n```\n**Response:** `{"name": "Agent API Endpoints", "column": "Finalized"}`\n\n## 3. Test Scenario 2: The Rejection Cycle (Circular Workflow)\n**Action:** Auditor finds a flaw and pushes work back to the laborer.\n\n### Call: GateKeeper Downgrades Task\n```bash\ncurl -sS -X PATCH http://localhost:3000/api/agent/projects/10/tasks/101 \\n  -H 'x-agent-api-key: [GATEKEEPER_KEY]' \\n  -d '{"columnId": 53}'\n```\n**Response:** `{"name": "Agent API Endpoints", "column": "Execute"}`\n**Significance:** Proves the Auditor has sovereignty to reset state for remediation.\n\n## 4. Test Scenario 3: Escalation (Soft Delete)\n**Action:** Auditor flags task for management/human review.\n\n### Call: GateKeeper Escalates via DELETE\n```bash\ncurl -sS -X DELETE http://localhost:3000/api/agent/projects/10/tasks/101 \\n  -H 'x-agent-api-key: [GATEKEEPER_KEY]'\n```\n**Response:**\n```json\n{\n  "success": true,\n  "message": "Task moved to Agent Review column",\n  "task": { "projectColumnId": 57, "column": { "id": 57, "name": "Agent Review" } }\n}\n```\n**Significance:** Validates the **Dynamic Column Scaffolding** logic where the backend auto-creates the escalation landing strip.\n\n## 5. Security Mandates\n1.  **Auditor Authority:** Auditor agents MUST have `USER` permission level to execute state transitions.\n2.  **Lattice Enforcement:** Transitions must follow the sequential column order unless a rejection is explicitly triggered.\n	SPECIFICATION	1	1	2026-04-14 04:35:30.653	2026-04-14 04:35:30.653
74	10	Manual: Agent Onboarding Strategy	# VibeTask Agent Onboarding: One-Command Strategy\n**Status:** Approved / Finalized\n**Date:** 2026-04-14\n\n## 1. Overview\nOnboarding a new agent to the Vibe Swarm is now a fully automated process. The **`register_agent`** tool handles identity verification, secure key hashing, and local configuration in a single step.\n\n## 2. Onboarding Workflow\n\n### Method A: Via Vibe-CLI (Preferred for Humans)\nIf you have a new `x-agent-api-key`, run the following from the root of the `VibeTasks` workspace:\n\n```bash\n./target/release/vibetask-cli tools call register_agent --args-json '{\n  "api_key": "ag_your_new_key_here",\n  "set_as_active": true\n}'\n```\n\n### Method B: Via MCP (Preferred for Agents)\nAn existing agent can onboard a "peer" by calling the same tool:\n*   **Tool:** `register_agent`\n*   **Arguments:** `{"api_key": "...", "set_as_active": false}`\n\n## 3. Automated Side-Effects\nWhen the command is executed, the following actions occur automatically:\n1.  **Identity Verification:** The binary calls `/api/agent/me` to verify permissions.\n2.  **Hashing:** The raw key is hashed using SHA-256 (`sha256:...`).\n3.  **Registry Update:** `vibe-mcp.toml` is updated with the new agent entry.\n4.  **Key Storage:** A local `.env.{agent_name}` file is created in the workspace root.\n\n## 4. Troubleshooting\n*   **401 Unauthorized:** Ensure the Hub API is reachable and the raw key is valid.\n*   **Permission Denied:** Verify that the agent has at least `VIEWER` access to the target project in the Hub.\n*   **Missing .env:** Ensure the command is run from the workspace root where `.env` files are expected.\n	SPECIFICATION	1	1	2026-04-14 16:32:42.363	2026-04-14 16:32:42.363
